**Finding submitted: IP — missing (fallback cure proposed).**

The Kubient MSA has no intellectual property or data-ownership article at all. Across the full document, the only IP-adjacent language is:

- **p0040** — Kubient warrants it "is the rightful owner or licensee of all intellectual property rights to the Service, the Auction Platform and products delivered hereunder" (Vendor-side ownership of everything delivered, with no licence back);
- **p0002 / p0021** — the only Customer licence is a "non-transferable, non-assignable, without right of sublicense, revocable" right to access the Auction Platform, revoked on termination;
- **p0025 / p0043** — AP is made "responsible and solely liable" for its Content and warrants it owns it, which allocates risk rather than retaining rights, and says nothing about Kubient's use of Requests or visitor data;
- **p0065** — trademarks only.

So AP has no rights in paid-for deliverables and no contractual limit on Kubient's use of AP data. I targeted the **fallback** rather than preferred ownership-assignment, because reversing deliverable ownership would require rewriting Kubient's section 9(b)(viii) warranty. The single inserted clause as section 6(b) carries exactly the eight fallback elements — Kubient keeps generic tools, know-how and the Auction Platform (so no conflict with the platform licence or p0040 as to its own technology); AP gets a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide, royalty-free licence to use, modify and sublicense deliverables for its business; and any Kubient licence in the Content or other data belonging to, supplied by or processed for AP is limited to processing solely to provide the Services. Redline validated (ok:true, minimality passed).
